@@ -0,0 +1,150 @@
+# RealYu customer funding candidate
+
+This is isolated development, not production migration acceptance. Default is
+disabled. It extends the native Sub2API gateway and `UsageBillingRepository`;
+there is no second relay process and no second personal customer wallet.
+
+## Wiring and activation
+
+- Create `repository.NewRealYuFundingRepository(sqlDB, enabled)` and call
+  `APIKeyService.SetRealYuFundingRepository` at startup, including when disabled.
+  A disabled repository rejects imported managed keys rather than silently
+  charging them with stock funding semantics.
+- Use `repository.NewUsageBillingRepositoryWithRealYuFunding(sqlDB, enabled)`
+  for native usage billing. Managed settlement requires a durable reservation.
+- `301_realyu_funding.sql` adds only the customer funding tables. Personal
+  wallets remain `users.balance NUMERIC(20,8)`; exact USD = quota / 500000.
+  No payment mutation, OAuth refresh, production DB, or production service is
+  part of this module's tests or activation.
+- `APIKey.user_id` is the acting user. `realyu_key_scopes` maps that key to an
+  actor, payer and optional team. Do not import a team key under its owner's
+  native user ID when a different member is the actor.
+- Scope/membership is read from PostgreSQL on every request, after native
+  authentication/IP/group checks. The returned APIKey is a request-local copy.
+  Both actor and payer accounts must remain active and not deleted for new
+  admission. Disabling a team owner stops new member spending and makes its
+  read-only allowance zero; earlier admitted receipts can still settle/refund.
+- New keys owned by `users.signup_source` `realyu_legacy` or
+  `realyu_username` receive a personal scope (`actor=payer=user`, no team) in
+  the same Ent transaction as key creation. Routing groups never infer team
+  membership. Caller-owned transactions remain caller-owned, and an existing
+  imported team scope is never overwritten. Native signup identities do not
+  receive an automatic RealYu scope.
+- A key belonging to either managed signup identity but missing its scope is
+  rejected by auth, continuation ownership and settlement, including with the
+  funding feature disabled. Older incomplete keys need an explicit audited
+  mapping repair; they cannot fall back to stock wallet billing.
+
+## Contracts and exact accounting
+
+- `Reserve` takes an absolute integer `TargetQuota`, not a delta. The request
+  identity and fingerprint are server-owned. Repeated same-state operations
+  are idempotent; a changed subject/fingerprint or changed final cost conflicts.
+- All operations serialize on the personal user row or independent durable
+  team row, then lock their reservation/subscription/member-period rows.
+- A personal request consumes its eligible subscription first, then its own
+  wallet only when permitted. Team historical quota is not spendable PAYGO;
+  a team consumes only its active weekly subscription and never any user's wallet.
+- `weekly_reset_at` is the next reset timestamp. Weekly allowance is the active
+  constraint; lifetime purchased total remains audit data. Paid fractional final
+  weeks receive `floor(weekly_amount * remaining_seconds / 604800)`.
+- Member caps apply on reservation. Team owners are cap-exempt. Historical
+  `opening_used_quota` is display-only. Actual completed usage above an estimate
+  is still recorded on its original funding principal (and may create allowance
+  debt); it is not silently discarded or moved into another person's wallet.
+- Late settlement/refund uses the immutable original subscription and member
+  period even after removal, closure or key deletion. It never consumes a new
+  week's member allowance. A settled request cannot be undone by a late failure
+  callback. A failed still-reserved request releases its funding once.
+- `UsageBillingRepository.Apply` settles funding, native key/account counters,
+  and native billing dedup in one SQL transaction. Native personal/subscription
+  deduction is skipped for managed requests. Failure in a later key/account
+  update rolls back the funding settlement and dedup as well.
+- Native price output is converted once through decimal to integer quota;
+  there are no epsilon comparisons. Request limits are int32 quota, cumulative
+  wallet/allowance bounds are 9007199254740991. Native customer key counters and
+  usage `ActualCost` use the same rounded quota amount. Upstream account cost
+  statistics retain their native upstream cost independently.
+
+## HTTP lifecycle and deliberate unavailable paths
+
+Managed HTTP Responses (including compact), Chat Completions, Messages and
+native Images generate/edit use the existing native handlers. The shared
+inflight helper performs a mandatory DB reservation even when stock Redis
+inflight reservations are disabled. Unknown pricing fails closed. Native user
+wallet eligibility is bypassed only for these mapped scopes; native key/rate/IP
+and model/group authorization still applies.
+
+Every admitted operation receives a fresh server reservation ID; client-supplied
+request IDs cannot cause new upstream work to be billed as a duplicate. Async
+usage workers preserve the reservation context, and managed billing is never
+dropped on a full worker queue: it falls back to bounded synchronous execution.
+The usage log's request ID links to the durable funding request.
+
+The initial allowlist intentionally rejects managed WebSocket, Google-native,
+batch and other protocol paths until each has one reservation per billable
+operation. A WS connection is not one turn. This is an incomplete migration
+capability, not a claim that stock Sub2API lacks those protocols. Legacy
+`GET /api/client/key-check` and model/usage reads don't reserve money and can
+work at zero wallet balance; they still validate live team membership.
+
+## Recovery and remaining release gates
+
+No usage task submitted means a failed handler can refund automatically. Once
+a task was submitted, a crash, timeout, panic or unknown SQL outcome preserves
+the durable reservation. There is no TTL-based refund of possibly consumed work.
+The `reserved` index supports operator review; automatic crash reconciliation,
+durable final-usage outbox/replay and a customer-facing pending state are not yet
+implemented. They are release gates, not hidden successful-settlement claims.
+
+HTTP cyber refusals with reported upstream usage use the same mandatory billing
+task/context as normal usage, including when the worker queue is full. A partial
+forward result is billed only by the normal path. Zero reported tokens may settle
+at zero under a known price. If settlement pricing is missing or invalid, managed
+OpenAI/Claude requests return a billing error and retain their reservation for
+reconciliation; an unknown price is never turned into a successful free bill.
+Explicitly configured free prices remain valid zero-cost settlements.
+
+Team/member management UI, complete subscription purchase/upgrade/renewal
+wiring, native portal funding display and
+per-turn WS funding also remain integration gates. Do not enable global customer
+traffic merely because isolated repository tests pass. Changing an enabled key's
+scope requires draining existing work; immutable admitted receipts remain payable
+to their original principal.
+
+HTTP continuation authorization also checks the saved owner key and current key
+against actor+team scope. Same-user personal/team A/team B are distinct; keys in
+the same personal or team scope remain interoperable. Missing scope, disabled
+funding, or DB errors reject managed continuation. This relies on scope bindings
+being immutable while traffic runs. Future online scope reassignment must store
+an immutable scope snapshot in response-owner state. Legacy bridge `resp_ry1_`
+translation and old Redis owner-state transfer remain unimplemented release gates.
+
+## Verification
+
+`go test -p 2 ./internal/service -run '^TestRealYu'` verifies exact conversion,
+request-local auth, native settlement command and failure lifecycle. Middleware
+tests use `-tags unit` and validate zero-wallet team actor identity, live revocation,
+read-only key-check and unsupported-path rejection. Handler tests verify mandatory
+queue fallback and context preservation.
+
+Real PG tests require an explicit private `REALYU_FUNDING_TEST_DSN` URL. The harness
+rejects non-loopback hosts and databases other than `realyu_funding_e2e`; each test
+creates/drops only its own synthetic schema. Run:
+
+```
+go test -p 2 -tags realyu_funding_pg ./internal/repository -run '^TestRealYuFundingPG' -count=1 -v
+```
+
+The fixture applies the real 301 migration over a minimal native billing schema.
+It verifies mixed payment, duplicate settlement, complete transaction rollback,
+double refund, concurrent wallet/key/member exhaustion, independent teams, zero
+actor wallet, closed/removed/deleted late work, cross-period accounting, allowance
+debt and fractional-week arithmetic. It does not prove full migration, full native
+schema boot, production runtime, paid upstream execution, or restart reconciliation.
+
+The additional key-enrollment fixture exercises the real Ent create on PG:
+managed personal defaults, unchanged native behavior, imported team preservation,
+scope-write failure rolling back the key, both context-owned and injected Ent
+transaction rollback, and missing-scope rejection in auth/continuation/settlement
+with the feature both enabled and disabled.
